<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Heat hydration principles"><div><strong>Heat raises strain</strong><span>Higher environmental heat can increase cardiovascular and thermoregulatory demands during exercise.</span></div><div><strong>Sweat losses vary</strong><span>Rate and sodium concentration change with athlete, intensity, climate and acclimatization.</span></div><div><strong>Cooling still matters</strong><span>A drink cannot substitute for pacing, shade, airflow, rest or emergency heat-illness response.</span></div></div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Why heat changes the plan</p><h2>Sweat cools the body, but it also moves water and electrolytes out</h2><p>Evaporating sweat is one of the body's main cooling mechanisms during exercise. In hot environments, especially with high work rates, sweat losses can accumulate quickly and make access to fluid more important.</p><p>Recent evidence also shows that heat and dehydration can alter substrate use during prolonged endurance work. These physiological effects do not create one universal prescription, but they reinforce the value of planning before the session starts.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media bhd-page__split-media--dianna"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0741/2170/7804/files/dianna_qualify_may2-26.jpg?v=1778093829" alt="Canadian sprinter Dianna Proctor competing outdoors" width="1080" height="1440" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Heat and electrolyte losses</p><h2>Hotter conditions can make fluid and sodium replacement more important</h2><p>Sweat losses vary across athletes, sessions and environments. Plan fluid access, cooling and electrolyte intake around the duration and conditions rather than a single universal target.</p><p>Longer hot sessions can make sodium-containing drinks more relevant, while total fluid, food, health conditions and pacing still matter.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Heat strategy</p><h2>Reduce the problem before trying to drink your way out of it</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Acclimatize</h3><p>Repeated heat exposure can improve thermoregulatory responses. Increase exposure progressively rather than jumping directly into full load.</p></article><article><h3>Shift timing</h3><p>When possible, move demanding sessions away from the hottest part of the day.</p></article><article><h3>Plan access</h3><p>Know where water and electrolyte drinks will be available before the session begins.</p></article><article><h3>Watch symptoms</h3><p>Confusion, collapse, severe weakness, loss of coordination or other concerning heat-illness signs require immediate action, not more training.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Best Hydrate in the heat</p><h2>Electrolyte-containing fluid can be useful when sweating is prolonged</h2><p>Best Hydrate provides water, glucose and listed electrolyte sources when prepared as directed. This can make it a practical beverage option during prolonged sweating.</p><p>Use an electrolyte-containing drink in the context of the session, conditions, sweat and your established routine. Longer, hotter and higher-sweat sessions increase the importance of planning fluid and electrolyte access.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Safety</span><h3>Heat illness is not a hydration marketing opportunity.</h3><p>Stop activity and follow appropriate emergency guidance when serious heat-illness symptoms appear.</p></aside></section>

